<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="71704" href="schedule/2021/spring/CWL/204/71704.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2021" href="schedule/2021.xml">2021</calendarYear><term id="120211" href="schedule/2021/spring.xml">Spring 2021</term><subject id="CWL" href="schedule/2021/spring/CWL.xml">Comparative and World Literature</subject><course id="204" href="schedule/2021/spring/CWL/204.xml">US Border Literatures and Cultures</course></parents><sectionNumber>AL1</sectionNumber><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>This course will consider the ways literary and cultural production can help us understand the emergence—and the emergency—of climate change. Through the study of fiction, nonfiction, poetry, and film, we will investigate the historical development, present crises, and possible futures of a warming world. Authors might include: Jesmyn Ward, Ben Lerner, Kathy Jetnil-Kijiner, Craig Santos Perez, and Amitav Ghosh.</sectionText><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>UNKNOWN</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2021-01-25Z</startDate><endDate>2021-05-05Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="OLC">Online Lecture</type><start>09:00 AM</start><end>09:50 AM</end><daysOfTheWeek>MW     </daysOfTheWeek><instructors><instructor lastName="Calderwood" firstName="E">Calderwood, E</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>